6. Alternative concept: String.join(), StringJoiner and String.format()
Instead of StringBuilder, Java provides other approaches to combine strings.

A. Using String.join()


java
static String buildUsingJoin(String[] fields) {
    return String.join("", fields);
}
String.join() combines all fields using the specified delimiter. It is suitable when all fields are already available in a collection or array.

B. Using StringJoiner


java
import java.util.StringJoiner;

static String buildUsingJoiner(String[] fields) {
    StringJoiner joiner = new StringJoiner("");

    for (String field : fields) {
        joiner.add(field);
    }

    return joiner.toString();
}
StringJoiner is useful when a delimiter, prefix or suffix is required.

C. Using String.format()


java
static String buildUsingFormat(String[] fields) {
    StringBuilder format = new StringBuilder();

    for (int i = 0; i < fields.length; i++) {
        format.append("%s");
    }

    return String.format(format.toString(), (Object[]) fields);
}
String.format() is useful for formatted ticket layouts, but it generally has additional formatting overhead and is not the best choice for high-volume concatenation.

