8. Multithreaded booking server: Design decision
The railway booking server has two different requirements.

Recommended for individual tickets
StringBuilder

Fast and efficient.

No synchronization overhead.

Each booking thread can create its own ticket independently.

Suitable because the ticket is not shared while being constructed.

Recommended for a shared log
StringBuffer

Synchronizes individual append operations.

Protects its internal buffer against concurrent modification.

Suitable when multiple threads append to the same shared log.

For a complete log entry, synchronize the entire group of append operations to prevent interleaving.

Example:


java
StringBuilder ticket = new StringBuilder();

ticket.append("PNR: 123456");
ticket.append("\nPassenger: Rahul");
ticket.append("\nCoach: B2");

StringBuffer sharedLog = new StringBuffer();

synchronized (sharedLog) {
    sharedLog.append("Ticket generated: ");
    sharedLog.append(ticket);
    sharedLog.append("\n");
}
For a high-throughput production server, a dedicated logging framework or concurrent logging queue would generally be preferable to maintaining one shared StringBuffer.

9. Final conclusion
The experiment demonstrates why choosing the correct String class is important in railway e-ticket generation.

For a small ticket, the performance difference is usually negligible.

For thousands of tickets containing many fields, StringBuilder is expected to outperform repeated String concatenation.

StringBuffer offers synchronization for shared mutable text but introduces additional overhead.

String.join() and StringJoiner are good alternatives for combining existing fields.

String.format() is more appropriate when formatted output is important than when maximum concatenation performance is required.

Final recommendation: Use StringBuilder for individual e-ticket generation and StringBuffer for a simple shared booking log when synchronized access is required.

The best choice depends on workload size, object creation, memory usage and concurrency requirements.


